(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-renal-physiology-2-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Renal II: Tubular Transport & Regulation — Quiz Set 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which nephron segment allows water to move out of the filtrate, with its length varying by nephron type?","choices":["Thin descending limb","Collecting duct","Thick ascending limb","Early distal tubule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thin descending limb allows water to move out of the filtrate, and its length varies depending on the type of nephron (cortical vs. juxtamedullary). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What do the thin limbs of the loop of Henle utilize to facilitate transport?","choices":["Direct innervation by the vagus nerve","The medulla's osmolality","Hormonal signals from the anterior pituitary","ATP-driven active transport exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thin limbs utilize the medulla's osmolality (the corticomedullary gradient) to facilitate transport, rather than relying on active transport. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Per the nephron comparison content, what distinguishes juxtamedullary nephrons from cortical nephrons regarding their loops?","choices":["Juxtamedullary nephron loops are shorter and stay entirely in the cortex","Juxtamedullary nephrons have longer loops that extend deeper into the medulla","There is no structural difference between the two nephron types","Cortical nephrons entirely lack a loop of Henle structure altogether"],"answer":1,"exp":"Juxtamedullary nephrons have longer loops of Henle that extend deeper into the medulla, related to the osmotic gradient that increases with depth in the kidney. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Per the summary table, the ascending limb of the loop of Henle reabsorbs which combination of water and NaCl?","choices":["High water, low NaCl","High water, high NaCl","None water, high NaCl","None water, none NaCl"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ascending limb reabsorbs none (no) water but high NaCl, consistent with its role in 'salt removal.' — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which nephron segment's key physiologic role is described simply as 'water removal'?","choices":["Ascending limb","Proximal convoluted tubule","Descending limb","Late distal convoluted tubule"],"answer":2,"exp":"The descending limb's key physiologic role is water removal (high water reabsorption, low NaCl reabsorption). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"The early distal tubule is described as 'not very permeable to urea' and also not permeable to:","choices":["Magnesium","Sodium ion","Chloride","Water (H2O)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The early distal tubule is not permeable to water, in addition to having low permeability to urea. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Extraglomerular mesangial cells, as part of the JGA, primarily contribute to:","choices":["Direct reabsorption of filtered glucose","Communication between JGA structures","Direct secretion of ADH hormone","Direct secretion of ADH into the tubule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Extraglomerular mesangial cells are part of the JGA and are involved in communication between its structures (macula densa and granular cells). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The macula densa's primary sensing function within the JGA is to detect:","choices":["Blood glucose concentration","Plasma osmolality directly","NaCl delivery","Arterial oxygen saturation"],"answer":2,"exp":"The macula densa senses NaCl delivery to the distal tubule, driving tubuloglomerular feedback and renin release. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"If NaCl delivery to the macula densa increases, what happens to renin release?","choices":["Renin release is unaffected","Renin release is suppressed","Renin is converted immediately to aldosterone","Renin release increases sharply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa suppresses renin release (the inverse of the low-NaCl-delivery response, which increases renin). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which cell type of the JGA releases renin?","choices":["Granular (JG) cells","Extraglomerular mesangial cells","Macula densa cells","Principal cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Granular (JG) cells, modified smooth muscle cells, are the ones that release renin. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Besides hormones, which factor listed in this lecture also regulates tubular reabsorption via arterial pressure?","choices":["Circadian rhythm alone","Only hormonal signals, with no pressure-based mechanism","Body temperature regulation","Glomerulotubular balance and pressure natriuresis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glomerulotubular balance, peritubular physical forces, the sympathetic nervous system, arterial pressure (via pressure natriuresis), and osmotic factors are all listed alongside hormones as regulators of tubular reabsorption. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which of the following decreases aldosterone secretion?","choices":["Increased Na+ concentration (osmolality)","Elevated circulating ACTH hormone levels","Elevated angiotensin II activity","Increased plasma potassium (K+) levels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Increased Na+ concentration (osmolality), along with atrial natriuretic factor (ANF), decreases aldosterone secretion. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Aldosterone increases K+ secretion in which cell type?","choices":["Intercalated cells","Principal cells","Podocytes","Proximal tubule cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Aldosterone increases both Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion in principal cells of the late distal and collecting tubules/ducts. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"During low blood pressure or decreased renal perfusion, angiotensin II preferentially constricts which vessel to preserve GFR?","choices":["Afferent arteriole","Renal vein","Efferent arteriole","Vasa recta"],"answer":2,"exp":"Angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, helping maintain glomerular hydrostatic pressure and preserve GFR during hypovolemia. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What happens if angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriole constriction becomes excessive?","choices":["The afferent arteriole compensates by constricting further","Renal blood flow increases without limit","GFR is preserved indefinitely with no downside","GFR can eventually decrease"],"answer":3,"exp":"While mild efferent constriction preserves GFR, excessive constriction can eventually decrease GFR. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Using the lecture's shorthand summary, PTH is described as doing what to calcium and phosphate?","choices":["Tosses both calcium and phosphate","Tosses calcium, preserves phosphate","Preserves calcium, tosses phosphate (secretion)","Preserves both calcium and phosphate"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the lecture's mnemonic, PTH 'preserves calcium, tosses phosphate' — i.e., promotes calcium reabsorption while promoting phosphate excretion (secretion). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"ANP's action on the collecting ducts includes inhibiting Na+ reabsorption, which has what effect?","choices":["No change in Na+ handling","Decreased Na+ excretion","Increased Na+ reabsorption elsewhere that fully compensates","Increased Na+ excretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"By inhibiting Na+ reabsorption in the collecting ducts, ANP increases Na+ excretion (natriuresis). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Besides reduced perfusion pressure and reduced Na+ delivery, what third trigger activates renin release via the JGA?","choices":["Increased plasma calcium","Increased plasma glucose","Parasympathetic stimulation via muscarinic receptors","Sympathetic stimulation via β1 adrenoreceptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation of the JGA via β1 adrenoreceptors is the third listed trigger for renin release, alongside reduced perfusion pressure and reduced Na+ delivery. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Where is aldosterone released from once angiotensin II binds its receptors in the RAAS sequence?","choices":["Adrenal medulla","Thyroid gland","Posterior pituitary","Adrenal cortex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Once angiotensin II binds its receptors, aldosterone is released from the adrenal cortex. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Full RAAS activation causes vasoconstriction of which arterioles specifically?","choices":["Afferent arterioles","Neither — RAAS only affects venous tone","Both afferent and efferent equally","Efferent arterioles"],"answer":3,"exp":"RAAS activation results in vasoconstriction of the efferent arterioles specifically, as part of the cascade's overall sequence of effects. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"The overall relationship of RAAS to blood pressure can be summarized as: decreased renal perfusion/NaCl delivery or increased sympathetic tone → renin release → ... → increased aldosterone, efferent constriction, ADH, and thirst → which final outcome?","choices":["Increased blood volume and pressure","No net change in blood volume or pressure","Decreased blood volume and pressure","Only heart rate changes, with no effect on blood volume"],"answer":0,"exp":"The RAAS cascade ultimately produces Na+ and H2O retention, which increases blood volume and blood pressure. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Before the countercurrent multiplier process begins (the theoretical starting point), the osmolality of the thin descending limb is:","choices":["Lower than both the interstitium and ascending limb","Already maximally concentrated","Equal to the interstitium and thin ascending limb","Impossible to define"],"answer":2,"exp":"Prior to multiplication, the theory assumes the osmolality of the thin descending limb is equal to that of the interstitium and thin ascending limb — the 'starting place' before the gradient develops. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why is it called the countercurrent 'multiplier'?","choices":["Because it happens only a single time, producing one large osmotic effect","Because it doubles the total volume of urine output produced with every single cycle","Because the single-effect and fluid-displacement steps repeat over and over, amplifying the gradient","Because it physically multiplies the total number of nephrons in the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"It's called a 'multiplier' because the process (single effect + fluid displacement) begins and continues repeatedly, amplifying the corticomedullary gradient over successive cycles. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What ultimately establishes the corticopapillary/corticomedullary osmotic gradient?","choices":["A single, isolated filtration event that occurs just once, very early in the glomerulus itself","The repeated single-effect and fluid-displacement cycle concentrating fluid toward the medulla and diluting it toward the cortex","The passive diffusion of plasma proteins directly into the surrounding medullary interstitium","The active, repeated secretion of glucose molecules directly into the collecting duct lumen itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"The repeated cycling of the single effect and fluid displacement — concentrating fluid as it descends and diluting it as it ascends — establishes the corticopapillary/corticomedullary osmotic gradient. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What happens to the osmolality of the collecting duct fluid when ADH is absent (-ADH)?","choices":["It increases substantially","It remains dilute","It matches the loop tip's ~1200 mOsm/kg exactly","It becomes iso-osmotic to plasma automatically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Per the lecture's osmolality table, in the absence of ADH the collecting duct fluid remains dilute, since water cannot be reabsorbed there without ADH. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"The vasa recta's descending limb, per the lecture's exchanger diagram, does what as it descends into the medulla?","choices":["Loses NaCl and gains water","Neither gains nor loses anything","Gains both NaCl and water simultaneously","Gains NaCl and loses water"],"answer":3,"exp":"The descending vasa recta gains NaCl and loses water as it travels into the increasingly concentrated medullary interstitium. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Secretion, as one of the basic mechanisms of urine formation, is defined as:","choices":["Useful materials being reabsorbed into the blood from the tubules","Plasma being filtered to remove water and small solutes","The remainder being excreted as urine","Substances being moved from the blood into the renal tubules"],"answer":3,"exp":"Secretion is the movement of substances from the blood into the renal tubules. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Secretion of H+, K+, ammonium (NH4+), and drugs/toxins occurs mainly in which segments?","choices":["Only within the glomerulus itself, nowhere else","Only within the thin descending limb segment","Only within the vasa recta blood vessels","The proximal and distal tubules and collecting ducts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Secretion of H+, K+, NH4+, and drugs/toxins occurs mainly in the proximal and distal tubules and collecting ducts, helping regulate acid-base and electrolyte balance. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Type B intercalated cells are responsible for:","choices":["Na+ reabsorption via ENaC","HCO3- secretion","ADH-dependent water reabsorption","H+ secretion and HCO3- reabsorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Type B intercalated cells secrete HCO3-, the opposite function from Type A cells. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Roughly what percentage of filtered HCO3- does the proximal tubule reabsorb?","choices":["~100%, with none left for other segments","~10-20%","~80-90%","~40-50%"],"answer":2,"exp":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs approximately 80-90% of filtered HCO3-, a process that requires carbonic anhydrase. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 56"}]};
